{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE PROVEN — a test-local race, NOT a store/backoff defect and NOT runner resource exhaustion\n\nClassification per AC #1: **test-timing**. Evidence from two CI runs plus a local deterministic reproduction of the exact error string.\n\n### The mechanism\n\n`stage_and_commit` runs under the exclusive store write lock, so `_git_add` and `_git_commit`\n(`src/rebar/_store/event_append.py`) both pass `force_reclaim=True` into\n`gitutil._with_index_lock_retry`. Under `force=True` the age gate is bypassed, so the test's\nplanted young lock is reclaimed on the FIRST retry — the write is never actually blocked for\nlong. Timeline:\n\n- t=0      test plants a young `index.lock`; test arms `threading.Timer(0.4, ...)`\n- t≈0      attempt 1 of `git add` fails on the lock; `_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock(force=True)` REMOVES the planted lock\n- t≈0.2    attempt 2 of `git add` succeeds\n- t≈0.2+   `git commit` runs and creates git's OWN REAL `index.lock`\n- t=0.4    the Timer fires and unlinks **whatever lock exists** — on a slow runner that is git's real lock, held mid-commit\n- git's `commit_locked_index` rename then fails: `fatal: unable to write new index file`\n\nThe timer body is `lambda: lock.exists() and lock.unlink()` — it has no identity check, so it\ncannot tell the planted lock from git's own. On a fast machine `git commit` completes before\nt=0.4 and the timer finds nothing; on the macOS py3.13 leg (23m24s vs ~12m for each Linux leg)\ncommit is still holding its lock at t=0.4.\n\n### Local reproduction (exact CI error string)\n\nReplicating the test's shape with the backoff widened so the timer deterministically lands\ninside `git commit`:\n\n    [0] FAIL StoreError: Error: git commit failed while holding lock:\n        fatal: unable to write new index file   timer_deleted_at=[0.4314896250143647]\n\n`timer_deleted_at` shows the timer unlinking a lock at t=0.431 — git's real one. This is the\nsame `StoreError` text reported from run 31338947918.\n\n### What is RULED OUT\n\n- **Not runner resource exhaustion.** All five failing job logs across the four cited runs were\n  grepped for `No space`, `ENOSPC`, `space left`, `Cannot allocate`, `resource temporarily` and\n  runner warnings: **zero hits**. Slowness is real (the macOS leg is ~2x the Linux legs) but it\n  is an amplifier of the window above, not the cause.\n- **Not a real backoff race (AC option (c)).** The production path behaves correctly throughout:\n  it force-reclaims the orphan, retries, and succeeds. The failure is caused entirely by the\n  test's own timer deleting a lock the code under test legitimately owns. No product change is\n  warranted.\n- **Not the lock-retry classification.** `fatal: unable to write new index file` is correctly\n  NOT in `_is_git_lock_error`'s signature set — it is a genuine write failure, and retrying it\n  blindly would be wrong.\n\n### Fix direction (test-only, behavior preserving)\n\n`gitutil` already ships the seam built for exactly this: `_retry_probe`, documented as\n\"a deterministic alternative to timer-based lock release\" — it fires after every attempt inside\n`_with_index_lock_retry`, letting the test release the planted lock only after the first failure\nis confirmed, with no wall-clock timer. Replacing the `threading.Timer` with that probe keeps\nthe test's oracle intact (a contended lock released mid-retry is ridden out and the write\nsucceeds) while removing the ability to delete git's real lock."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786500486031682001,"uuid":"2256ca0e-e712-41b0-8e70-75d29bb9cde6"}